WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.870
Now that Spider-Man: Homecoming's home entertainment
release is out, there's a stack of bonus material

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.970
including new deleted scenes and footage.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:12.539
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm revealing some very cool Spider-Man

00:00:12.539 --> 00:00:16.150
Homecoming deleted scenes and explaining some
of the easter eggs and missing details we

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.570
never got to see in the theatrical release.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.970
I'm also starting a new Funko Pop giveaway
on this video and for a chance to win, make

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.300
sure you're subscribed to my channel and leave
a comment below with your thoughts on the

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.619
deleted scenes or anything Marvel.

00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:32.181
Quick warning, obviously there are spoilers
ahead for anyone who's not yet seen the movie.

00:00:32.181 --> 00:00:38.380
The first deleted scene on the digital release
comes via an extended director's cut of Peter

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:42.650
Parker's vlog-style behind-the-scenes video
of what happened on his trip to Berlin during

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.050
the events of Captain America: Civil War.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.100
The extended version includes some great additional
footage of what Peter got up to the night

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.330
after the airport battle in Berlin when he
was so hyped up that he decided to put on

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:57.430
his Spidey suit and go partying in the German
capital.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:16.520
"I should really go to bed, but I don't want
to."

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.259
"Shhh."

00:01:18.259 --> 00:01:22.499
This additional footage of Spider-Man swinging
between buildings with a girl explains set

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:27.079
photos that emerged during the movie's production
which showed Spider-Man swinging from a helicopter

00:01:27.079 --> 00:01:31.139
as he looked to be saving a red-haired girl,
which at the time led to some speculation

00:01:31.139 --> 00:01:33.549
that Mary Jane was going to appear in the
movie.

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:37.610
The extra footage also seems like a fun way
for the movie to pay homage to the web-slinging

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:42.479
superhero from Sam Raimi's trilogy who swung
between New York skyscrapers with ease, which

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:46.189
was something the filmmakers otherwise made
a conscious decision to step away from in

00:01:46.189 --> 00:01:47.380
Homecoming.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.179
And that might be the reason this scene ended
up being deleted because Tom Holland's Spider-Man

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:56.850
in both Civil War and Homecoming hasn't yet
mastered all his abilities and actually finds

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:01.770
it pretty scary when he has to scale the Washington
Monument, so including scenes of him happily

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:06.520
swinging through Berlin might have felt inconsistent
with his character at this point.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.289
It's still a shame we couldn't see this cut
though as it also finishes with a funny scene

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:14.659
where Peter gets busted by Happy Hogan who
discovered what he got up to thanks to the

00:02:14.659 --> 00:02:16.930
front pages of a German newspaper.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.760
And that feels like a nice way to end the
scene especially given that the filmmakers

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.670
set up Peter's vlog as an updated take on
his photojournalism in the comics and previous

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:27.520
movies.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.200
After the school bus leaves for the Academic
Decathlon, it passes by a road sign that reads

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.620
"Triskelion Cleanup", a little easter egg
to the SHIELD headquarters located on Theodore

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.060
Roosevelt Island in Washington DC.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:43.060
The building was severely damaged during the
final showdown between HYDRA and SHIELD in

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:47.300
Captain America: The Winter Soldier and there's
a deleted scene in Homecoming which revisits

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.680
the damaged site.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.650
In the scene, Peter, Ned and Michelle see
the damaged Triskelion building as the school

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.750
bus drives over a bridge.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:58.540
"Oooh, what is going on?"

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.049
"Damage control."

00:03:00.049 --> 00:03:03.659
"They're still cleaning up from when Captain
America took down those Skycrap carriers for

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:04.659
no reason."

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:07.550
"I'm pretty sure Cap was just trying to save
us from tyranny."

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:08.550
"Eh.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.569
That's what they want you to think."

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:15.329
It's a humorous moment that shows how different
characters in the MCU view Captain America.

00:03:15.329 --> 00:03:18.280
Ned thinks he's a hero, but Michelle is way
more sceptical.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.090
This would have been an interesting easter
egg to include but it looks like it was likely

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:26.400
cut for time, which will have also saved a
little cash as the CGI for the damaged Triskelion

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:27.989
building wasn't completed.

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:32.689
By the way, the scene also included a little
joke about Thor not having a phone, a nice

00:03:32.689 --> 00:03:36.959
nod to the funny moment in the Thor mockumentary
where Thor complains to Bruce Banner about

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:40.909
Tony Stark and Captain America not asking
him to help out with Civil War.

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:42.939
"Was that Tony Stark?"

00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:43.939
"No."

00:03:43.939 --> 00:03:44.959
"Was that Thor?"

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:45.959
"Nooo.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:49.019
I don't think Thor uses a phone."

00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:50.030
"Good point."

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:51.290
"Here, talk to Thor."

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:52.290
"Yep."

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:53.290
"He's been sorta waiting to hear from you."

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:54.290
"I have him right here."

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:55.290
"Oh you can't right now."

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.909
"He doesn't know how to get in touch with
you."

00:03:56.909 --> 00:03:58.230
"I don't have a phone."

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.719
"He doesn't have a phone."

00:03:59.719 --> 00:04:02.200
"Send a raven."

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.090
Homecoming already teased the existence of
Miles Morales in the MCU in the scene where

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:10.689
Donald Glover's character Aaron Davis gets
caught by Spidey in a car park and mentions

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:12.670
he has a nephew in the neighbourhood.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:16.600
But there's a little extra footage of Donald
Glover that makes the existence of Miles Morales

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.669
even clearer.

00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:24.699
"Excuse Me!"

00:04:24.699 --> 00:04:28.240
"A human being needs help."

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:29.240
"Yeah, sorry Miles.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.370
I ... I'm not gonna make it.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.340
Yeah I'm just stuck."

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:38.150
If this moment was meant to complete the movie's
scene with Glover, I can see why it was cut

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.169
as it slows down the film's momentum just
for the sake of making a more obvious reference

00:04:42.169 --> 00:04:43.199
to Miles Morales.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:47.650
However, I wonder if it might have originally
been planned as a humorous extra post- or

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.030
mid-credits scene?

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:52.990
In the comics, Davis is the uncle of Miles
Morales who, like Peter Parker, was bitten

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:57.810
by a radioactive spider turning him into a
famous web-crawling superhero.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:02.160
Miles Morales will be getting his own animated
Spider-Man movie released by Sony, however,

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.560
the Morales easter eggs in Homecoming could
also be setting up a possible appearance in

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.389
the MCU somewhere down the line.

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:14.400
Talking of possible deleted after-credit scenes,
it looks like the Vulture's mid-credits scene

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.470
was cut back as Laura Harrier who played Adrian
Toomes' daughter, Liz, has revealed that she

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.550
filmed a scene where she meets her father
in prison.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:27.069
If you notice, the mid-credits scene actually
finished with a prison guard calling out to

00:05:27.069 --> 00:05:30.250
the Vulture that his family had arrived to
see him.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:31.680
"Toomes!

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.380
Your family is here."

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:41.090
This is interesting because Harrier has said
she wishes the scene had been included and

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:45.229
she's also said that she'd like to see her
character Liz get powers as she does in the

00:05:45.229 --> 00:05:48.090
Ultimate comics as the superhero Firestar.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.780
In fact, I wonder whether that additional
moment in the mid-credits scene could have

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.170
set that up in some way?

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:57.510
There was only a little bit of footage from
the Midtown High School news channel in the

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.120
final cut of Homecoming.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.539
"This past weekend, Midtown's academic decathlon
team defeated the country's best to win the

00:06:03.539 --> 00:06:04.669
national championship."

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:06.509
"Later that day they also defeated death."

00:06:06.509 --> 00:06:09.139
"As you know we made out alive and that's
the important thing."

00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:12.120
"Couldn't bear to lose a student on a school
trip."

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:16.099
"Not again."

00:06:16.099 --> 00:06:19.340
But there's a load more additional news channel
footage with Betty and Jason that we never

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:20.340
got to see.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:25.530
It's got plenty more hilariously awkward lines
and deliberately bad editing, but interestingly,

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:30.669
it also includes some easter eggs and additional
info on small plot points from the movie.

00:06:30.669 --> 00:06:36.000
"Questions remain this morning as authorities
piece together the alarming events at this

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.389
weekend's homecoming dance."

00:06:37.389 --> 00:06:42.660
"An unidentified male individual assailant
was taken into custody after police found

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.180
him attached to the side of a bus with Spider-Man
webs."

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.700
"OK"
"To those of you ..."

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:54.430
"To those of you who took inappropriate selfies
with the perpetrator webbed to the bus, you

00:06:54.430 --> 00:07:02.860
have 24 hours to remove them from all your
social media accounts."

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:07.340
At the end of that clip, we can see that Tiny,
one of Peter's classmates, is now in possession

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:11.350
of Shocker's gauntlet, which it looks like
he nabbed from the villain after Spider-Man

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:14.659
webbed Shocker to a school bus during their
fight in the movie.

00:07:14.659 --> 00:07:19.069
This moment could just be a bit of fun or
it might tease Tiny's character actually using

00:07:19.069 --> 00:07:23.800
this high-tech weapon in the sequel, especially
given that, in the comics, Tiny has been known

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:25.669
to bully Peter.

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:33.050
"In sports ... the girls basketball team takes
on the Flatbush Asgardians this Thursday night."

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:34.979
"Let's show our tiger pride."

00:07:34.979 --> 00:07:37.860
"Go get 'em tigers!"

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:38.860
"Roar."

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:43.270
The Flatbush Asgardians is an amusing Thor
easter egg which shows that Thor and the Asgardians

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:45.599
seem to be quite popular on Earth now.

00:07:45.599 --> 00:07:49.670
We already saw a curious reference to Thor
in the final movie in the shape of the Korean

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:51.181
Church of Asgard which was next to the Thai
restaurant where May and Peter ate.

00:07:51.181 --> 00:07:55.610
And, of course, Betty's "Go get 'em, tigers"
is a nice easter egg to MJ's "Go get 'em,

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:59.080
tiger" which she said to Peter Parker at the
very end of Spider-Man 2.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:03.550
There's another funny segment called "Under
The Mask" which speculates on the real identity

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:04.550
of Spider-Man.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:08.659
"Up next on Under The Mask, who is Spider-Man?"

00:08:08.659 --> 00:08:09.659
"Who is Spider-Man?"

00:08:09.659 --> 00:08:11.800
"I dunno who he is, but I have my suspicions."

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:13.289
"It's a mystery, always will be!"

00:08:13.289 --> 00:08:17.680
Michelle has her suspicions, which implies
she thinks it's someone she knows, and at

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.650
the end of the movie, she joked that she thought
Peter was hiding something, so I'm thinking

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:26.080
this is quite likely a tease to her uncovering
Peter's secret identity in the sequel.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.909
"Don't forget today's Sokovian Relief lunch."

00:08:28.909 --> 00:08:31.900
"Enjoy a $5 all-you-can-eat paprikash buffet."

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:34.130
"All proceeds go to charity."

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:37.209
This clip gives us two tasty call-backs to
MCU movies.

00:08:37.209 --> 00:08:42.320
First of all, it's clear that there are ongoing
efforts to raise money to help Sokovia after

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.240
the destructive events of Avengers: Age of
Ultron.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:49.240
And secondly, the paprikash meal the school
canteen is serving is a nice nod to the dish

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:53.620
that Vision prepared for Wanda in Captain
America: Civil War, and, remember, that Wanda

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:57.050
herself also originates from Sokovia.

00:08:57.050 --> 00:09:01.220
Happy Hogan gets a short comedic deleted scene
where he calls up his mother after he's finished

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:04.360
arranging the move of Avengers tech to the
the team's new facility.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:05.360
"Yeah, hey ma."

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:07.420
"Yeah, it's Harold."

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:10.160
"Yeah, it's done."

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:14.220
"Yeah, Tony trusted me and I took care of
it."

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:15.220
"Me.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:16.420
Yeah mom me.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:17.680
Is that so hard to believe?"

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.890
"That I could accomplish something.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:24.370
I ..."
"I'll call you back."

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:28.570
It looks like this cut scene is trying to
build up some backstory for Happy in the MCU,

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:30.930
showing how he feels he has to prove himself
to his mum.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:34.760
And while it's humorous, I can understand
why this moment was probably cut for time;

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:39.060
after all, it doesn't add an awful lot and
may have even undercut the drama of the fight

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.640
scene going on in the cargo plane at the same
time.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:46.130
There are a few other deleted scenes which,
although mildly interesting, were likely trimmed

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:50.029
to avoid padding out the final cut which was
already over 2 hours.

00:09:50.029 --> 00:09:53.971
One is an additional scene where Peter's classmates
try to convince him to go to the Academic

00:09:53.971 --> 00:09:54.971
Decathlon.

00:09:54.971 --> 00:09:56.124
"You can't bail a week before nationals.

00:09:56.124 --> 00:09:59.680
We don't have anyone as strong as you in physics."

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:01.100
"But you have Flash!"

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.100
"You got me!"

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.100
"Everyone prefers Peter!"

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:04.100
"Yeah."

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:07.300
"Look, they're not gonna do a physics challenge
2 years in a row."

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:08.709
"Guys I'm sorry.

00:10:08.709 --> 00:10:10.120
I gotta ... Guys!"

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:11.120
"Screw you!"

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:12.120
"You suck!"

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.120
"Seriously"

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:16.310
I'm glad this was cut as it doesn't add much
compared to the better paced scene of the

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:20.670
decathlon team practising which introduced
each of Peter's classmates in a much funnier,

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.630
more interesting way.

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:26.670
Other scenes cut to trim the fat include various
moments of Spider-Man running and swinging

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:30.380
around Brooklyn; a scene of Adrian Toomes
on the phone to his daughter Liz; and a scene

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:34.110
of Peter and Ned doing their secret handshake
on the street again.

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:38.870
Captain America's PSAs were something a lot
of fans enjoyed and there are many more which

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:43.430
aren't on the digital release, but which are
expected on the Blu-ray, including one about

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:44.800
brushing your teeth.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.130
In the meantime, there's an additional one
about hot lunches that wasn't included in

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:50.230
the movie but is on the blooper reel.

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:56.130
"Hi, I'm Captain America, whether you're a
student or a soldier, there's one thing that

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:59.230
will give you an edge, a hot lunch!"

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:03.520
Now, what do you think of those deleted scenes
and do you wish any had been included in the

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:04.520
film?

